# bug-a-a-managed-local-that-survives-a-yield-is-released-at-every-yield-on-every-cross-target
#
# A stackless generator step function RETURNS at every yield and is re-entered
# at the next one, so its locals are NOT going out of scope on that path --
# they are the generator's live state, checkpointed into the heap instance and
# restored on resume. EmitManagedLocalCleanup (x86-64) has always exited early
# for that reason; the five cross arms of EmitManagedLocalCleanupForTarget
# never got the guard, for ANY kind, so every string / variant / promo-int /
# record / dynamic-array / class local of a step function was released at each
# yield.
#
# `acc` and `parts` must SURVIVE the yield -- a local rebuilt inside the loop
# body cannot see this at all, because its release lands after its last use and
# the program stays correct either way. Measured before the fix, all three
# cross targets, exit code 0 and a WRONG VALUE:
#
#   x86-64  ['x|1|0', 'xx|2|0,1', 'xxx|3|0,1,2', ...]   (correct)
#   i386    ['x|1|0', '1|1|0,1',  '2|1|0,2,2',   ...]
#   arm32   same as i386
#   aarch64 same as i386
#
# `acc` comes back as the loop counter's string and every earlier element of
# `parts` is overwritten -- the freed blocks handed out again. A closely
# related shape (a string local, no list) SIGSEGVs on i386 and arm32 instead,
# so the class of failure spans silent-wrong-value to crash. x86-64 was correct
# throughout: the cross rows are the guard, the x86-64 row is only the oracle.
#
# NO promo-int local here on purpose. The first draft carried one and it stayed
# wrong on i386/arm32 after this fix, for an unrelated reason that reproduces
# outside any release path: a promotable-int local in a generator truncates to
# 32 bits on those two targets, identically on pin v403 and at HEAD --
# bug-a-a-promotable-int-local-in-a-generator-truncates-to-32-bits-on-i386-and-arm32.
# Leaving it in would have made this row fail for a defect it does not guard.
#
# Expectations are CPython's own output.

def gen(k):
    acc = ""
    parts = []
    i = 0
    while i < k:
        acc = acc + "x"
        parts.append(str(i))
        yield acc + "|" + str(len(acc)) + "|" + ",".join(parts)
        i = i + 1


out = []
for v in gen(6):
    out.append(v)
print(out)
print(len(out))
